[Music]  we're gonna get started everybody thank  you so much for joining us today I hope  you enjoyed the keynote a lot of great  speakers there and we have some  excellent content for you today my name  is Brian singer I am the head of cloud  commerce product cloud commerce  encompasses our GCP marketplace product  as well as orbit era white label  marketplaces and multi cloud billing we  also have Roger pulse with us today  Roger is the EVP and chief innovation  officer at Pitney Bowes and he's going  to talk a little bit about Pitney Bowes  is digital transformation and efforts to  build an ecosystem so I think one of the  exciting things about cloud is I've seen  it mature is that the strategies around  why we're using cloud and why it's  important to our companies have really  evolved in the beginning we were moving  to cloud to save money right we could  pay by the drink we could use services  only as we needed them and it was a lot  quicker to provision a VM to get  something up and running as we've gotten  a little bit smarter about how we use  cloud security has actually become a  driver right what Google is able to do  with security and its data centers and  what we're able to do with increasing  levels of automation is secure our  workloads in the cloud to a level that  we've never been able to do before and  as we've solved these problems right and  as we've been able to get better  economies of scale out of cloud we've  actually started to unlock new products  in ecosystems so cloud and some of the  characteristics of cloud have actually  allowed us to create products that could  not have existed before cloud cloud  platforms evolved and that's really what  we're gonna talk about today is right  how do we actually monetize a product  that we've built in cloud so we'll look  a little bit about pricing strategies  and things like that and then how do we  build an ecosystem around a platform  because as we as we've seen some of the  most valuable companies and products  that are out there today actually are  products that have enabled an ecosystem  right so we think about building a  product for cloud we have to answer some  key questions one how do we monetize it  what's the right way to ask our customer  to pay for that service and every  company out there and you'll hear from  Roger right Pitney Bowes you know  started as a shipping company and you'll  see every company is actually evolving  into more of a traditional ISV software  provider as our data is moving to cloud  and as more of the services that we  provide to our customers are digital  everyone needs to start thinking about  how do we become a software vendor  ourselves right and inherent to that is  understanding the billing models that  are available to us in cloud and which  models are right for us as we build  these services and this is important to  everybody in a company from the beady  guy who's going out and selling it to  the developer who's actually building  the product because you actually have to  think about the pricing in the billing  model as you build out the product and  you'll see why in a few slides  so in the cloud there's a couple of  billing models that have become you know  very common and there's some good  reasons for that first is a subscription  model which everyone is probably  familiar with we all have subscriptions  in our day to day lives the key things  to think about when it comes to  subscription are that it's based on a  cap set of usage so you set the  threshold for what a customer can use  right typically within a plan and you  know that's basically you know the  amount that you're going to charge them  in any given month for use of that  subscription works great for non  variable workloads where it's not  changing that much if you think about  like network usage for example that  could change a lot over the course of a  month so it's not great to have a  subscription to a network you know to a  certain amount of bandwidth but  consumption on the other hand  usage-based pricing has become much more  common for cloud services right we use  consumption pricing for GCP for virtual  machines we use consumption pricing for  storage and this is based on the actual  usage of a product and we think about  this as being best for variable  workloads when you don't necessarily  have a flat amount of usage over many  months or in a given month and you want  to build a customer for exactly what  they're using  so looking a little bit more closely at  subscription right  it's that fixed monthly price usually  prorated to a given date and there's a  few actions that are usually available  to a customer when they have a  subscription such as upgrading or  downgrading between plans um there's  actually a lot of complexity here that  you need to think about when building  out your service because the upgrade and  the downgrade could be manual you could  say to a customer we're gonna cut you  off unless you go upgrade your plan it  could also be automated based on certain  business logic or rules that you want to  put in place right you could say we're  going to let you use up to a certain  amount and if you go over that we're  going to put you into another tier  alright so when you think about  monetizing your service you have to make  sure you have a platform that can  support whatever model it is that you  choose the other thing that's very  interesting about subscriptions is that  you can have what we call a one to many  relationship between the entitlement  which is the thing that the customer is  actually buying from buying from you and  the pricing plan that they're that  they're using what this means is you  could have for example one plan that is  a yearly commitment that's paid monthly  and you could have another plan that is  a yearly commitment that's played you  paid yearly all right both for the same  entitlement in either case the customers  actually gonna be paying a different  amount most likely but they're paying  for the same thing and this one too many  relationship is really core to how  subscriptions work so moving on to  consumption billing there's a few  different types of consumption billing  that you could insert that you could  build into a service that you have on  demand is obviously the most simple it's  just what I'm using times the price for  each item in the case of GCP there's a  price for a VM for an hour all right you  take the number of hours of that VM and  multiply it by the price and that gives  you your total cost and so the benefit  of this is it's very simple easy to  understand right the drawback is that  it's also a little bit inflexible which  is why we have what we call tiered  pricing for consumption as well and with  what this means is that as you start to  use more right you pay less and you see  this more in storage and net  working and things like that where you  know you kind of expect to derive some  economies of scale you can see the  example here where as we go from you  know 20 widgets to 30 widgets to 30 plus  the actual price per widget is coming  down and the the cost right in each tier  there gets lower and lower the the nice  thing here is that as the customer is  using more right they're getting the  benefit of that discount which is  actually going to keep them consuming  from you if there's other alternatives  one of the most popular models that we  see out there though is what we call a  base Plus consumption model this  combines elements of subscription  billing with consumption billing if you  think about a committed use discount in  GCP for example right there is  essentially a subscription a commitment  and then there is a per widget fee or  you know per hour fee if you go over  that the benefit of this is that it  allows you to charge the customer for  the you know basic level of what they're  going to use right you can probably  forecast that they're gonna have some  basic level of usage and you can lock  that in you can give them a discount for  that and then anything over that right  they're still able to consume but  they're just gonna pay a little bit more  and this tends to be the most flexible  and actually confers a lot of benefits  to everybody one of the things that you  need to think about though if you are  going to do consumption billing is what  are the metrics you're going to bill  against some of the common metrics that  we see are time so  number of hours for example that  somebody's using your service data how  much data is stored sorry how much data  is being transferred storage or just a  count of something right it could be the  number of connections that the users  made made to the service the key is that  you know this is where the the business  really has to come together with the  engineering team to figure out what are  the right metrics and then how do we  count them and how do we monetize on  them for our service so just putting it  all together real quickly if whether you  have a subscription or a consumption  plan  right you actually have to figure out  what is the route to market then for  this product so you have a few different  options you could sell it directly to an  end customer you could also bring it to  market through a public cloud  marketplace like TCP marketplace and you  can also sell it through your own  channel no matter what the billing model  is though you have to make sure that  billing model fits with the go to market  that you're choosing for your product so  great you've created a wonderful new  product on cloud you've added  monetization you've picked your route to  market now comes the fun part  where you actually get to go build an  ecosystem around this product now I  think it's important to note that an  ecosystem is not an app store and just  because you have an app store it doesn't  mean you have an ecosystem I actually  found this this definition in a paper as  I was preparing for this talk and I  really thought it did a good job of  outlining what an ecosystem actually is  so I'm gonna read it real quick a  software ecosystem is a set of actors  functioning as a unit and interacting  with a shared market for software and  services right these relationships are  frequently underpinned by a common  technological platform or market and  operate through the exchange of  information resources and artifacts so  the really interesting thing to me is  that all ecosystems have this common  technological platform in common right  and this is really core to actually  being able to build out an ecosystem so  if you think about what has to be in  place before you can even think about  creating an ecosystem around your  product you have to have this common  tech platform it also really helps to  have a stable customer base so if you  don't have customers that are using your  product it's very hard to attract other  companies to build around the platform  that you've created so it's a question a  lot you know that we get a lot of the  time is you know should I work on  building the ecosystem first or building  the customer base first I would say that  building the customer base is very very  important because it's hard to attract  that ecosystem if you don't have it in  the first place your customers also need  to be engaged with your platform an  ecosystem will only really  if they believe that they can make money  by working with you and with your with  your customers and if the customers  aren't engaged with the platform it's  hard to actually get that flywheel  spinning and then finally you have to be  able to create a trusted relationship  with your customers what you're doing  effectively by creating an ecosystem and  maybe wrapping an app store around it is  saying that you actually believe these  solutions are good right there they're  your customers are going to be able to  use them they're going to work etc etc  and if you don't have that trusted  relationship with your customers it's  very difficult to get them to start  using third-party software in terms of  the actual product requirements couple  things have to be in place first of all  you have to be able to support the  exchange of information in today's world  that usually means that your platform  has open api's that third parties can  use so if you have data right that you  know it's part of your platform that  you're making available to your  customers you want other third parties  to be able to provide services on top of  that you have to have some way for them  to get at that data so open API is  extensibility are very important in  creating an ecosystem a couple great  examples everyone's probably familiar  with Google Play I think actually sundar  talked about the Android ecosystem a  little bit it's more than just obviously  the App Store it's all the telcos all  the customers all the app app makers  right and all of these entities are  working together to deliver value to the  end-user the common tech platform in  this case is obviously the Android  operating system right and you can kind  of see how some of the elements in that  definition applied to Android GCP is  another example of an ecosystem right  we're all here we're all part of that  ecosystem and some of the things we're  trying to do to help that ecosystem  organize and form are things like GCP  marketplace which we've made available  so just a quick bit like why are  ecosystems important I think some of  this goes without saying but for one it  increases loyalty and and stickiness  from the customers right if they if  they're getting products not just from  you but from other entities right and  you're facilitating that they're gonna  stick with you over the long term second  it helps fill gaps in the platform  offering it's very difficult to have a  solution that solves all the problems  that your customers want you to solve  right you will not be able to hire  enough engineers to make that happen but  if you have a robust ecosystem they can  fill in these gaps and then finally it  gives you avenues for additional  monetization and value creation so it  goes without saying if you have build a  marketplace people are going to be  buying things from that marketplace and  whoever is facilitating that that that  transaction is able to take a little bit  you know a little bit of that value that  they're creating  so the ecosystems can actually form  without you doing anything right you'll  see if you have a great product and  customers come into that product other  third parties are going to want to get  engaged but for it to really organize  and monetize effectively it helps to  actually have a place to for customers  to go to discover solutions and procure  them and so on so in terms of actually  operationalizing we look at it from two  sides the partner sides so these are the  entities that are creating value-added  solutions around your products right and  in the case of them there's really three  things to think about onboarding and  this means bringing them into your  ecosystem right getting the contracts  signed you know doing doing sort of the  the actual product integration work  certification so that means looking at  the solutions that you're bringing in to  the end of the plat into the marketplace  and actually running them through some  sort of certification process so that  when your customers are using them they  can be relatively assured that they're  going to work right and they can trust  the solutions that are available from  your partners and then finally go to  market alright it's not enough to just  usually put solutions out there  your partner's you know whoever is  participating your ecosystem is going to  need help selling on the customer side  there's some basic things that customers  need in order to make the ecosystem  effective they have to be able to find  the solutions that are out there all  right this is where the App